WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.485
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:13.390
The lecture processing
project is really

00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:16.750
all about letting people
search inside videos.

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:19.210
Conventional search
engines are text-based,

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:22.510
so Google or Yahoo,
things like that.

00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:24.847
You type in your text query,
and you find the documents

00:00:24.847 --> 00:00:25.930
that you're interested in.

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:29.980
That's great, but with
video, you can't do that.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:32.890
And we're living in an era
where there's more and more

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:35.840
videos are becoming available.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:37.930
But it's hard to find
for little snippets

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:40.060
that you might be searching
for inside the video.

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:44.170
So we're using speech
recognition information

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:48.310
retrieval technologies to help
process the videos so that then

00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:51.940
people can search them using
conventional text-based

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:53.170
mechanisms.

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:57.250
The way we process a lecture
is a series of steps.

00:00:57.250 --> 00:01:01.150
First, we actually chop
it up into little 10

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:02.480
second increments.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:05.410
And then we run a phonetic
recognizer over these chunks

00:01:05.410 --> 00:01:07.600
to find out where the speech is.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:10.000
Once the speech has
been recognized,

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.290
we put all the
pieces back together,

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:16.870
and then we can index it with a
conventional text-based search

00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:17.470
engine.

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:21.430
My group starts processing these
materials once we have actually

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:25.420
speech transcribed into text.

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:29.630
And what we're doing is trying
to find structure in it.

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:33.060
Because if you have
textual material written,

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:35.612
you have sentences, and
paragraphs, and sections.

00:01:35.612 --> 00:01:37.570
When you look at the
transcript, you don't even

00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:39.410
have sentence boundaries.

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:42.220
So in order to be able to
support efficient retrieval,

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:45.070
you need to know what
is a proper unit.

00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:49.260
So when a user asks about
a recursive function,

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:51.370
know which spot to show to him.

00:01:51.370 --> 00:01:55.090
So our goal is to find the
structure in the text--

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:56.870
in the transcript.

00:01:56.870 --> 00:01:58.540
So we have a publicly
available site.

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:02.680
And when you go there,
it has a video field.

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:04.507
But it also has a
conventional text field

00:02:04.507 --> 00:02:07.090
where the user can type in the
keywords they're interested in,

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:09.490
or perhaps select a
subset of the courses they

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:10.720
want to search on.

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:13.780
So if they type in
the query terms,

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:16.630
they'll get a set of
hits showing the lectures

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:19.600
that somewhere in the lecture
the keywords that they

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:20.888
were looking for occurred.

00:02:20.888 --> 00:02:22.930
And if they find something
they're interested in,

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:25.750
they can click on any
word at any point,

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:29.560
and it will start the video
up at that exact spot.

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:33.400
And below it, you'll see
a synchronized transcript

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:36.340
moving along with the video,
just showing the output

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:38.530
of the speech recognizer.

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:41.290
The left side of
that piece of plastic

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:43.740
is only positively charged.

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:44.790
So here it is.

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:48.340
The system so far had
around 2 million hits.

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:50.860
We were surprised to find
how much people like it,

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:53.620
and they really wanted
to have it expanded

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:55.600
in the full
interaction so they can

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:57.080
download their own lectures.

00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:59.350
Right now we have a
pre-recorded set of lectures.

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:00.740
We just did access those.

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:03.940
And we got requests from
all parts of the country,

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:07.300
ranging from doctors
to legislators,

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:10.180
who were asking us
to use similar tools

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:14.020
for their own video libraries.

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:18.980
I think the technology has
two major applications.

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:20.480
One is for accessibility.

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:22.900
So for example, for
hearing impaired students,

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:26.090
video by itself is
not that useful.

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:28.490
So if you have text
to go along with it,

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:30.250
that would be very
useful for people.

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:32.680
Also very useful for non-native
speakers who might have

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:34.660
trouble following the speech.

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:37.000
There is another, I think,
interesting direction

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:41.830
is to try to sort of
solicit help from users

00:03:41.830 --> 00:03:43.160
to improve the transcripts.

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:45.250
Because recognition
would never be perfect,

00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:47.490
like in the next 10 years.

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:49.090
There always will
be some mistakes.

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:51.960
And it would be really cool
if the same way as Wikipedia

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:54.500
is created, when just users
are editing their materials,

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:58.750
if they can do the same
corrections for lectures.

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:01.300
And maybe add their notes,
and whatever information

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:02.050
they found useful.

00:04:02.050 --> 00:04:07.870
So it is like sort of a source
which is evolving and improving

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:09.660
all the time.

